  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given that, $f(x)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1+x^2}}$\nFor injective: Let $x_1, x_2 \\in \\boldsymbol{R}$ such that $f\\left(x_1\\right)=f\\left(x_2\\right)$\n$\\Rightarrow \\frac{x_1}{\\sqrt{1+x_1^2}}=\\frac{x_2}{\\sqrt{1+x_2^2}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{x_1^2}{1+x_1^2}=\\frac{x_2^2}{1+x_2^2}$\n$\\Rightarrow x_1^2+x_1^2 x_2^2=x_2^2+x_1^2 x_2^2 \\Rightarrow x_1^2=x_2^2$\n$\\Rightarrow x_1=x_2$\nSo, $f(x)$ is injective.\nFor surjective: Let $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1+x^2}}$\n$\\Rightarrow y^2\\left(1+x^2\\right)=x^2 \\Rightarrow y^2+y^2 x^2=x^2$\n$\\Rightarrow x^2\\left(1-y^2\\right)=y^2 \\Rightarrow x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y^2}{1-y^2}}$\n$\\Rightarrow \\frac{y^2}{1-y^2} \\geq 0$\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/bitsat/t_rmbkCKJ_uT0LHAFaMmlnHgnSPUTErY1TwW2TVaYho.original.fullsize.png\"/>\n$\\therefore y \\in(-1,1)$\nSo, $f(x)$ is not surjective.",
    "image": null
  },
